example logs
==============================================
disk- ------------reads------------ ------------writes----------- -----IO------
       total merged sectors      ms  total merged sectors      ms    cur    sec
nvme0n1  72247   9525 11385380   94606  24563  23577  589668   24428      0     32


| Column    | Meaning                             |
| --------- | ----------------------------------- |
| `total`   | Total completed I/O requests        |
| `merged`  | Requests merged by kernel           |
| `sectors` | Sectors read/written                |
| `ms`      | Time spent doing I/O (milliseconds) |
| `cur`     | I/O currently in progress           |
| `sec`     | I/O per second                      |

----------------------------------------------------
Reads
----------------------------------------------------
reads total = 72,247
sectors read = 11,385,380
read time = 94,606 ms (~94 seconds total)

Average read latency = 94,606 ms / 72,247 reads ≈ 1.3 ms per read

✅ 1.3 ms is FAST (NVMe is usually 0.1–2 ms)

1 sector = 512 bytes ≈ 5.8 GB read from disk (That is a large amount of data being read repeatedly.)



----------------------------------------------------
Writes
----------------------------------------------------
writes total = 24,563
sectors written = 589,668
write time = 24,428 ms (~24 seconds)

Average write latency = 24,428 / 24,563 ≈ 0.99 ms per write
✅ Also fast

----------------------------------------------------
IO rate
----------------------------------------------------
sec = 32 IOPS


✅ Disk is not slow
❌ But disk is heavily used for reads


----------------------------------------------------
Check disk queue depth
----------------------------------------------------
cur = 0

This means:
No pending I/O
Disk is keeping up with requests
No backlog
❌ If disk were slow → cur would grow (>1)




----------------------------------------------------
Disk analysis:
➡ Disk is healthy but busy

“Disk is slow, we need bigger IOPS” ( ❌ Wrong conclusion)
“Memory pressure is forcing disk reads” (✅ Correct conclusion)
----------------------------------------------------
Very high I/O time per operation
Growing I/O queue
High I/O wait (wa) --> If disk were slow → wa would be 20–50%+
Requests piling up (cur > 0)












